          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-14251",
          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{\u2032}=\\frac{2x+3}{y}.$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & =\\frac{2x+3}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+3}{y} \\\\ y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int (2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+3)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & ={x}^{2}+3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}={x}^{2}+3x+C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & ={x}^{2}+3x+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+6x+2C \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x+2C} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{2{x}^{2}+6x+K}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=2C.$"
