          ":question/id": "b5785d45-e79c-5188-8c0a-65a380b832ea",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-168070",
          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation in part 1 for $s\\ne 0.$\n\n$s\\,=\\,($\n{{field-1}}$+K)^{-1}$\n$\\,\\,$where $K$ is an arbitrary constant.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =2{s}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}s}{{s}^{2}} & =2\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ -\\frac{1}{s} & =2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $s=s(t)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $s.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{s} & =2t+C \\\\ s & =-\\frac{1}{2t+C} \\\\ s & =\\frac{1}{-2t+K} \\\\ s & =(-2t+K)^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K=-C$ is an arbitrary constant."
