          ":question/id": "b071164a-d7dc-50ad-9600-bbe303de9fa0",
          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-168084",
          ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation in part 1.\n\n$p\\,=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$+C)$\n$\\,\\,$where $C$ is an arbitrary positive constant.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{{e}^{p}} \\\\ {e}^{p}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =3 \\\\ \\int {e}^{p}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{p}\\text{d}p & =3\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {e}^{p} & =3t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a perfectly valid general solution. However, if we want to write $p=p(t)$ explicitly, then we can solve for $p.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{p} & =3t+C \\\\ p & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(3t+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary positive constant."
