            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186068448,
              ":question/id": "23d66cc8-8e5a-5a33-a7a1-c43f5facdcbc",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-10833",
              ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $y' + 2xy = 0$ for $y\\neq 0.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2xy & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2xy \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y}\\,\\text{d}y & =-2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n\\ln \\vert y \\vert = -x^2 + C.\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y = y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061|y| & =-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}+C} \\\\ |y| & ={e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\cdot {e}^{C} \\\\ |y| & =K{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K = e^C$  and $K > 0.$ \n\nWe can drop the absolute value bars by allowing $K$ to be either positive or negative. Therefore, the most general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ke^{-x^2}.\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "74b784e1-ad0e-5cf2-8b02-604a7098c6a9",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Finding General Solutions by Rearranging Then Integrating"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186068842,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186068449,
              ":question/id": "32040154-373e-5a10-a460-1d17f6989da0",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-10849",
              ":question/problem": "Given that $x,y \\geq 0,$ separate the variables in the following differential equation: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2xy}\n$$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "A differentiable equation is *separable* if it can be written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x)g(y).\n$$\n\nIf an equation is separable, we say that the variables are *separated* when it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{g(y)} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = h(x).\n$$\n\nNotice that our differentiable equation is separable since it can be written as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} =  \\sqrt{2x} \\cdot  \\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{y}} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\sqrt{2x}\n$$"
            },
            ":knowledge-point/id": "c0d908f1-591e-5cb6-afc5-fa7ec44ec326",
            ":knowledge-point/title": "Separating the Variables in a Differential Equation"
          },
          {
            ":db/id": 17592186068844,
            ":knowledge-point/canonical-example": {
              ":db/id": 17592186068451,
              ":question/id": "d53c6172-978d-57c7-b1f0-e56e892e48a9",
              ":question/math-academy-id": "e-10997",
              ":question/problem": "Find the general solution to the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    \\dfrac{x}{y}.$",
              ":question/worked-solution": "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\cancel{\\text{d}x}}\\,\\cancel{\\text{d}x} & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int y\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+2K \\\\ {y}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C= 2K.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\ny^2 = x^2 + C .\n$$"
            },
